proposals.idea:
Build a replacement model whose nodes are typed geometric variables and whose edges are typed geometric maps, and render it as a per-prompt attribution graph.

(1) GEOMETRIC UNITS. Each unit u has:
- a gate a_u(x) ≥ 0;
- a coordinate z_u(x) on a manifold M_u drawn from a typed library: point (an ordinary linear feature), interval, circle S^1, torus T^2, sphere S^2, simplex, or a finite graph;
- a smooth low-rank embedding D_u: M_u → R^d, built from a Fourier or B-spline basis restricted to a rank-r_u span (r_u ≤ 16).
Reconstruction is x ≈ Σ_u a_u D_u(z_u). A standard SAE is the special case where every M_u is a point, so the method strictly generalizes SAEs.
Units are initialised by grouping latents of existing dictionaries (SAELens, Gemma Scope, circuit-tracer CLTs). The grouping combines decoder cosine similarity, co-activation, Ising-style anti-correlation and Jacobian coupling. Each group's topology is chosen by description length (MDL), with persistent homology (ripser) as a prior. Units are then fine-tuned end to end against downstream KL.

(2) GEOMETRIC TRANSCODERS. For each MLP layer and attention head, fit sparse typed edges. Output coordinates and gates are low-complexity functions of a few input units, z_v = φ(z_u1, z_u2, ...). φ is chosen from a ladder ordered by description length:
- isometries of M (rotation, translation);
- group operations (angle addition on S^1 or T^2);
- monotone 1-D warps;
- threshold or projection readouts (the linear-boundary motif);
- bilinear forms;
- a small-MLP fallback, charged in bits.
For attention, QK is fit as a kernel on M_q × M_k. The 'twist' motif is k(θ_q, θ_k) = A·cos(θ_q − θ_k − δ) for circles, or −(z_q − z_k − δ)^2 for intervals. OV is fit as a map M_k → M_out.
Training uses three losses: local reconstruction, end-to-end downstream KL, and an interventional-consistency term in the style of interchange intervention training (IIT). The consistency term patches a unit's coordinate in the real model and requires the replacement model to predict the downstream coordinates, so edges are causal rather than merely correlational.

(3) GEOMETRIC ATTRIBUTION GRAPHS. For each prompt, nodes are the active units with their coordinates. Each edge carries a typed label (e.g. 'rotate by δ=+4 chars', 'add angles', 'threshold at z=0.3') and an attribution, computed as the directional derivative of the target logit along the manifold tangent.

(4) BOOKKEEPING. All descriptions are scored in bits (parameters at fixed precision plus residual code), so GeoTrace and a CLT graph can be compared directly on the complexity tax. The tool also reports a 'geometric fraction' per layer and task: the share of faithful computation that non-point units and non-linear-lookup edges carry.

Score each candidate abstraction (high-level model H, alignment map φ) by total description length:

L = L(φ) + L(H) + L(D_cf | H, φ)

D_cf is a stream of interchange-intervention outcomes from the real model. It is coded prequentially, as in online-code MDL: φ is trained on blocks 1..k, then the model's actual counterfactual output distribution on block k+1 is coded under H's prediction. Prequential coding is largely robust to how φ is parameterised, and it charges automatically for maps that memorize.

NULL CALIBRATION. The key quantity is the Abstraction Gain ΔL in bits per counterfactual, measured against three nulls:
(i) the same architecture at random initialization, which is Sutter's adversary;
(ii) a control-task version of H with its variable semantics permuted (Hewitt and Liang, https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.03368);
(iii) the maximal-capacity RevNet map.
Confidence intervals come from bootstrapping over counterfactual pairs. A claim that 'network N implements H with format F' is accepted only if ΔL is significantly above all nulls.

FORMAT LADDER. The search covers an explicit ladder of alignment-map families:
- neuron-aligned;
- rank-k linear (DAS);
- affine with offset, which MAxBench found decisive;
- manifold charts (S^1 angle within a 2-D subspace, helix, sphere, interval along a curve);
- magnitude or norm codes ('onion');
- piecewise-linear with k regions;
- RevNet of depth n.
The MDL-optimal rung is reported as the variable's encoding format.

AMBITIOUS EXTENSION: MDL algorithm discovery. Search jointly over H in a small typed DSL of causal programs. Variables are categorical, ordinal or cyclic; operations include copy, compare, add-mod, lookup and gate. Return the network's shortest faithful algorithm with its Pareto frontier. GeoTrace units (proposal 1) can serve as candidate low-level variables.

The tool interoperates with interp-lab's evidence grades: a claim is 'validated' only with positive ΔL against the nulls.

proposals.minimal_demo:
(a) Defuse the dilemma. Reproduce Sutter et al.: random-init Pythia-70m / GPT-2 small reach high IIA on IOI with RevNet maps. Success: Occam Causal Abstraction (OCA) gives random-init models ΔL ≈ 0 (95% CI includes 0) while trained models get ΔL ≥ 0.5 bits per counterfactual. Linear DAS IIA alone cannot separate these cases cleanly once maps are allowed to grow.

(b) Planted formats. Train small transformers and GRUs with interchange intervention training (IIT, https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.00826) to encode a target variable as linear, circular, magnitude/onion (reproducing Csordás's GRU) or XOR-like, with 10 seeds each. Success: OCA picks the planted format in ≥90% of cases. Baselines fail: linear DAS mislabels non-linear formats through low IIA, and unconstrained RevNet 'finds' every format, including in random nets.

(c) LRH census. Cover 15–20 known variables in GPT-2 small, Pythia-410m and, with a GPU, Gemma-2-2b or Qwen-0.5B:
- IOI subject/indirect-object name and position;
- greater-than start year;
- day-of-week and month offsets;
- entity-binding order ID;
- line position;
- gender, sentiment, country→capital, number parity.
Report format, ΔL and CIs. Track the trajectory over Pythia training checkpoints.

(d) Predictive validity. Use the out-of-distribution correctness protocol of Huang et al. 2025. Success: abstractions selected by OCA give higher OOD AUROC than abstractions selected by maximum IIA.

proposals.why_new_understanding:
It would give the first direct, quantitative measurement of superposition inside real LMs:
- how many directions a residual stream actively defends;
- how many can be active at once before defense collapses;
- how both quantities scale with width, depth, model size and training. Pythia checkpoints allow tracing when error correction emerges, which links to the superposition explanation of scaling laws in https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10465.

It also provides a criterion, grounded in the model rather than the data, that can grade every feature or manifold any method proposes. This directly addresses the 'activation space is doomed' critique and the seed-instability problem. It turns 'is this geometry real?' into a measurable property: the model stabilizes it. If error correction turns out to be weak or absent in small models, that is itself a significant result for superposition theory.

proposals.minimal_demo:
(a) Toys with ground truth (CPU):
- Toy Models of Superposition (TMS) with n features in d dimensions, sweeping sparsity;
- a computation-in-superposition toy, both the L^4-trained version and a Hänni-style construction;
- a circle-feature toy.
Success criteria:
- the privilege score separates true features from mixtures and random directions with AUROC ≥ 0.95;
- the measured capacity knee matches the analytic or simulated prediction within 20%;
- the normal-to-tangent gain ratio is well below 1 for the true manifold and about 1 for a random 2-D plane.

(b) GPT-2 small and the Pythia suite (70m, 160m, 410m, 1.4b) with public SAEs. Score 2–5k latents per model.
Pre-registered predictions:
- privilege correlates with cross-seed stability (Spearman ρ > 0.3), with ablation effect on loss, and with steering success;
- low-privilege latents are enriched for surface-form triggers;
- random-init models show p ≈ 2 and no capacity knee;
- capacity per dimension rises over training checkpoints.

(c) Manifold attractor test on GPT-2 day and month circles (Engels et al.) and on the Pythia-410m line-position helix. Success: the normal-to-tangent gain ratio is below 0.5 in trained models and about 1 in random-init models, with bootstrap CIs.

(d) Privilege-anchored SAE versus standard SAE at matched L0. Success: higher cross-seed reproducibility and equal or better downstream KL.

proposals.idea:
Replace the scalar latents of a (cross-layer) transcoder with typed chart atoms. Each atom carries a sparse gate plus a low-dimensional coordinate, and the edges between atoms become plottable functions.

1. Atom form. Atom k has:
   - a sparse gate g_k(x) ≥ 0 (BatchTopK or JumpReLU across atoms);
   - a chart encoder c_k(x) of type point (0-d, which recovers an ordinary latent), interval (1-d), circle or torus (angle via atan2 of a learned 2-D projection), or a general 2–3-d chart;
   - a smooth decoder D_k(c) = Σ_m B_m(c) V_{k,m}, using a Fourier basis on circles and tori and B-splines on intervals, so the output curve has a closed form.
   The transcoder output is y ≈ Σ_k g_k(x)·D_k(c_k(x)) + W_skip·x.

2. Type and dimension selection by minimum description length (MDL). The loss is downstream KL (or MSE) plus bits per token, where bits = gate-index bits + coordinate bits at a fixed precision + an amortised parameter cost (the MDL-SAE framing, https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11179). An atom is promoted from point to chart only when it pays for itself in bits.

3. Promote-and-merge training, which scales by reusing existing dictionaries:
   - start from an ordinary TopK transcoder or CLT (interp-lab or Gemma Scope 2 checkpoints);
   - group latents that co-activate exclusively and whose decoders lie on a smooth low-dimensional curve (geodesic or Isomap test);
   - fit an initial chart and decoder to each group;
   - fine-tune end to end, with periodic split and merge moves.

4. Variable graph. For an upstream atom j and a downstream atom k, push j's decoder curve D_j(c) through the frozen linear pathways (residual stream plus attention OV with frozen patterns, as in attribution graphs) and read off k's coordinate and gate. The result is an edge function f_jk: c_j → (c_k, g_k), a one- or two-dimensional curve you can plot and fit with symbolic regression, for example θ_out = θ_a + θ_b or θ_out = θ_in + 2π·n/7. These edges are independent of the input, which is the global connectivity CLTs lack. Supernodes come out of the method as atoms rather than being picked by hand.

5. Deliverables: a library that trains atlas transcoders and atlas CLTs, converts existing SAEs and CLTs, extracts variable graphs, and provides coordinate-steering and coordinate-patching tools.

Model each activation as ordinary sparse atoms plus a small number of bound role–filler pairs, with roles, fillers and the binding operator all learned without labels.

1. Activation model:
   x ≈ b + Σ_j a_j d_j + Σ_{(r,f)∈S(x)} c_rf · M(ρ_r ⊗ φ_f)
   - roles ρ_r ∈ R^p form a small dictionary (R ≈ 16–512);
   - fillers φ_f ∈ R^q form a large dictionary (F ≈ 4k–64k) and can be tied to an ordinary SAE's latents;
   - M is a learned, low-rank-factored binding map. It contains the matrix, tensor and HRR/circular-convolution binding schemes in Wattenberg & Viégas's taxonomy as special cases.
   A standard SAE would need up to R·F latents to cover all role–filler combinations; this model pays only about R + F parameters plus the cost of M.

2. Encoder by amortised unbinding:
   - a role-presence head selects the active roles (TopK);
   - for each active role, an unbinding map U_r x gives evidence for the bound filler, followed by TopK over fillers;
   - an optional second explain-away pass subtracts the decoded bindings and re-encodes the residual.

3. Objective: reconstruction (optionally downstream KL, as in end-to-end SAEs) plus MDL-style bit costs. Gauge-fixing regularisers (unit-norm roles, orthogonality of M's factors) reduce rotation and scale ambiguity between roles and fillers.

4. Two built-in causal tools:
   - binding swap: exchange the fillers of two roles in code space, decode, patch into the model, and predict the output change;
   - role transplant: move a role code to another entity.

5. A cheap diagnostic on existing dictionaries: test whether subsets of a trained SAE's decoder vectors form a low-rank role × filler tensor. In other words, is a given SAE secretly a TPR with each combination stored as a separate latent?

proposals.minimal_demo:
Stage A: synthetic ground truth, CPU.
- Reproduce Hönig et al.'s 2-layer attention-only entity–relation retrieval model.
- Pass criteria:
  - recovered role and filler factors have cosine above 0.9 with the probe-derived address components;
  - binding swaps in Binding-Autoencoder (BAE) code space flip the retrieved answer to the swapped entity in more than 90% of cases;
  - a TopK SAE at matched bits per token stays below 50% on the same swap test, confirming its reported failure.

Stage B: OthelloGPT (Li et al. 2023, https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.13382, synthetic-game checkpoint, 8 layers, d = 512), CPU or small GPU.
- Train an unsupervised BAE on residual activations.
- Pass criteria:
  - about 64 square roles and {mine, theirs, empty} fillers emerge;
  - subspace alignment with Lee et al.'s supervised TPR factors is above 0.8;
  - board-state accuracy decoded from BAE codes is at least as good as the SAE board-state metrics of Karvonen et al., with at least 3× fewer active codes;
  - flipping one square's filler changes legal-move logits as linear-intervention baselines predict (Nanda et al. 2023, https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.00941).
- Also run the 'secretly a TPR' tensor-rank test on existing OthelloGPT SAEs. Pass: a role × filler factorisation of the relevant latents reaches R² above 0.8.

Stage C: real LLMs, one GPU.
- Models: Pythia-1.4b, plus Gemma-2-2b or Qwen-0.5b.
- Train the BAE on a mix of generic web text and binding prompts (Feng & Steinhardt templates and the Gur-Arieh task suite).
- Pass criteria:
  - the role atoms correspond to ordinal / binding-ID positions;
  - role-code swaps between two entities switch the queried attribute in at least 70% of cases, compared against supervised binding-ID patching (upper bound) and SAE latent swaps (lower bound);
  - the roles persist when the BAE is trained on generic text only.
- Also report the share of residual variance carried by bound terms at matched bits, and how much of Engels-style linearly predictable SAE error the bound terms remove on entity-dense text.

The idea fails if role and filler structure does not beat a plain SAE at matched bits on OthelloGPT and the synthetic task, or if swaps fail causally.

proposals.idea:
Build two things and use them together: a suite of ground-truth model organisms, and a single decomposition that chooses among representation types by description length.

(1) Known-World Transformers. A generator samples text from a latent-variable program with declared variable types:
- a hierarchical discrete taxonomy (which induces absorption and hedging);
- cyclic variables (circles);
- monotone counters (intervals);
- entity–attribute bindings that are declared and later queried;
- an embedded Mess3/RRXOR-style hidden Markov model, whose belief states form a fractal simplex;
- Dyck stack depth.
The exact Bayes-optimal posterior over all latents is computed at every position with the forward algorithm, so we know what a near-optimal predictor must represent. Transformers with 2–8 layers are trained to within about 2% of optimal loss, and supervised regression confirms each latent is present (R² above 0.9).

(2) A typed MDL decomposition: one dictionary whose atom types compete on two-part code length (bits for model + gates + coordinates + residual at a fixed precision). The types are:
- point (SAE latents);
- chart (manifold atoms, from proposal 1);
- bound (role ⊗ filler, from proposal 2);
- simplex (barycentric belief atoms);
- dense (always-on low-rank subspace, cf. dense latents, https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.15679);
- a Gaussian noise floor.
Moves such as promote, demote, merge and split are accepted only when they reduce total bits. That gives a principled answer to 'what L0 and width' and 'is this a manifold or N features'.

(3) Null calibration and consensus:
- run the same pipeline on random-init networks and on networks trained on corpora with shuffled latents;
- report only structure that beats both nulls (permutation p-values) and is reproduced across at least 3 seeds.

(4) The census. Apply the calibrated pipeline to GPT-2 small, the Pythia suite and TinyStories models. For each layer, report the share of variance and of downstream KL carried by each type, with seed confidence intervals and curves against bit budget, plus a dark-matter account of which types absorb the SAE residual.
